# /etc/sec/sec.env — every secret this service needs, and nothing else.
#
#   install -o root -g sec -m 0640 sec.env.example /etc/sec/sec.env
#
# Read by systemd through `EnvironmentFile=` (deploy/rhel9/systemd/sec-backend.service) or by
# compose through `env_file:`. Both hand these to the process as environment variables, which is
# what application.yaml resolves "$SEC_NEO4J_PASSWORD" from.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY NOT .bashrc, AND WHY NOT THE UNIT FILE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ~/.bashrc is wrong for a team for four separate reasons, and only the first is obvious:
#
#   1. It is per-person. Six developers means six copies drifting apart, and the answer to "what
#      is the JIRA token in production" becomes "whose shell are you in?".
#   2. systemd does not read it. A unit started at boot gets none of it, so a service that works
#      when you start it by hand fails after a reboot — the worst possible failure mode, because
#      it happens weeks later to somebody else.
#   3. It is exported to every child process of every interactive shell, including anything you
#      pipe from curl. A secret in the environment of a shell is a secret in the environment of
#      whatever that shell runs.
#   4. It is readable by the account itself, so it survives that account being repurposed.
#
# Nor do these belong in the unit file: `systemctl show sec-backend` prints Environment= lines to
# any user, and unit files are world-readable by design. EnvironmentFile= is read by pid 1 as
# root, so 0640 root:sec really does keep it from everyone else.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ROTATION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Edit this file, then `systemctl restart sec-backend` (compose: `docker compose up -d backend`).
# There is no reload — the process reads its configuration once, at startup, deliberately.
#
# Rotating SEC_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET means changing it in Keycloak first (Clients → sec-backend →
# Credentials → Regenerate), then here, then restarting. Between those two steps every login
# fails, so do it in a window.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS NOT HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The DOORS push client's secret (sec-doors-push, ADR 0020) is deliberately absent: the backend
# never calls that client's token endpoint, it only verifies tokens the exporter already holds.
# That secret belongs on the machine running the exporter, not on this one.

# --- Neo4j -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The single service account (Community has no RBAC — CLAUDE.md §7). Unset means the application
# refuses to start, which is intended: a cockpit without a graph is not a cockpit.
SEC_NEO4J_USER=neo4j
SEC_NEO4J_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME

# --- Keycloak / OIDC ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clients → sec-backend → Credentials → Client secret. Also unset-means-refuse-to-start:
# authorization is infrastructure every deployment needs, not an optional source, and a backend
# that started without it would be unreachable rather than insecure — but unreachable silently.
SEC_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=CHANGE-ME

# --- JIRA (optional) ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A personal access token for Data Center, or an API token for Cloud. Empty is a working state:
# /api/v1/jira/** answers 503 with a reason the settings page shows, and nothing else is affected.
SEC_JIRA_TOKEN=

# --- Overrides (optional) --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything in sec.yaml can also be set here, which is what a container without a writable
# filesystem uses. Prefer the yaml file when you have one — it carries the comments.
# SEC_OIDC_ISSUER=https://sec.example.corp/auth/realms/sec
# SEC_OIDC_CALLBACK_URL=https://sec.example.corp/api/v1/auth/callback
# SEC_BEHIND_PROXY=true
